      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "Yes, this would really help, and I recently found a similar platform to VRChat that allows Cutout shaders on Quest using VRoid models. It's called Cluster, and my avatar was easy to upload to there, with no needing to change the material to something that would break the avatar, it just uploaded, cutout shaders and all. It might be safe to make it so that if you DO use a cutout shader, the texture resolution must be 1024 or below to make it so it isn't as performance heavy, or make it so that only the toon unlit shader has the ability to render in cutout, since after all, most of the avatars that can't be uploaded to VRChat Quest are VRoid made avatars. I even have a model from Battle Arena Toshinden, a PlayStation 1 game, and I can't get it onto Quest because the hair uses a cutout shader. The only thing holding me back from uploading avatars to Quest is the lack of support for cutout shaders, and if there was support, you bet ALL my avatars would be cross platform, and cut down for Quest so people can stop seeing me as a generic blonde woman. I want to be seen as my avatar, and while imposters DO help a little, I don't know how well it would work with a plus sized avatar, or a chunkier avatar in general, since it doesn't look fully 3D, and appears to have sprite renderers on a model that is broken up into segments, similarly to Mario 64, or maybe it's animated vector graphics, I don't know. This is honestly frustrating though, I will agree. The majority of avatars that aren't cross platform are probably PC only due to not having support for Cutout shaders.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
