      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{5acad9ecc9dba010c9c3ec68|full_name} This may be true, yet at the same time, so is rendering realtime lights, and particles on avatars for Quest. A lot of avatars cannot be ported to Quest due to cutout shader limitations. Unless you're uploading a sphere layered like an onion with all layers having the leaf block texture from Minecraft, I doubt it will kill performance too bad. I can't create impostors for all of my avatars, as I have to pick and choose which avatars are made into imposters, and when they're as chunky as mine, it causes some really disturbing approximation that looks terrifying tbh. Perhaps a good middle ground would be that you choose between uploading with cutout shaders (but not transparent) or you can upload with particles, and lights, but you aren't allowed to upload it with both. You can choose one feature, or the other. This would most likely also make the Creator Companion feel like a better tool in general. Currently, with how VRChat handles the Quest version, it feels more like you're supposed to first make sure your model would run on Quake 2's engine first, then you can be sure that you can use it as a Quest avatar, which heavily limits the creative freedom of people playing the Quest version. Even if I trusted Meta, I still wouldn't want to play the Quest version of VRChat because of the very limited avatar choices, and the difficulty of actually getting an avatar onto the Quest version of VRChat without having unsightly black boxes all over the model.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
